   "text": "This is atorvastatin calcium: two atorvastatin carboxylate anions and one Ca\u00b2\u207a ion.\n\nEach anion has these parts:\n- A central pyrrole ring whose nitrogen carries the side chain.\n- On the ring carbon next to the nitrogen: a 4-fluorophenyl group.\n- On the next ring carbon: a phenyl group.\n- On the next ring carbon: a C(=O)NH-phenyl (anilide) group.\n- On the last ring carbon, also next to the nitrogen: an isopropyl group.\n- On the nitrogen: a \u2013CH2CH2\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH2\u2013CH(OH)\u2013CH2\u2013COO\u207b chain.\n\nThe two hydroxyl-bearing carbons are drawn with hashed bonds. I took the (3R,5R) configuration from the known form of atorvastatin rather than deriving it from the drawing, and wrote the SMILES in the PubChem pattern for that configuration. I did not re-derive the CIP assignment from the hashes.\n\nSMILES: CC(C)c1c(C(=O)Nc2ccccc2)c(-c2ccccc2)c(-c2ccc(F)cc2)n1CC[C@H](C[C@H](CC(=O)[O-])O)O.CC(C)c1c(C(=O)Nc2ccccc2)c(-c2ccccc2)c(-c2ccc(F)cc2)n1CC[C@H](C[C@H](CC(=O)[O-])O)O.[Ca+2]"
